  the confusion on my face because he  handed me medical gloves and said just  go look at it and please tell us what we  should do I turned my attention to prion  ax as I introduce myself my name is Cal  weirdo and I am a nurse even the words  felt foreign and uncomfortable as I  added them however the eerily silent  plane and Brianna's pained whimpers left  no space for self-doubt or hesitation I  began to feel a strange rush of energy  and an instinct I did not even know  existed begin to take over with a sense  of authority words came out of my mouth  call 9-1-1 and taxi back to the gate  immediately I peeled back the napkin she  clutched over her eye as thoughts of  incompetence were replaced by adrenaline  fueled deliberation and focus with each  panicked exhalation her ruptured implant  used further out of her eyeball I heard  a fake cough behind me that morphed in  the sound of uncontrolled retching as I  peered to my right I saw a first-class  passenger vomit into a white paper bag  I turned my focus back on Brianna the  cut over her orbital bone was pouring  blood before I was even cognizant of my  plan I said Brianna we're gonna get  through this I will be here for you the  whole time I just need you to keep your  head as still as possible and take deep  breaths I reached for the gauze pads  from the first-aid kit that had been  placed at my feet and took hold of  Brianna's face with both hands  I needed to stabilize her head with each  small movement the implant became more  dislodged with the other hand I began  applying pressure on the wound to the  left of her eye the gauze pads changed  from a white to a bright red in seconds  medical knowledge rattled in my head as  I tried to sift through facts and bring  them to the front of my mind I took my  left hand off of her head and checked  her pulse 120 okay good  I thought who sacs flooded my mind the  head is extremely vascular and bleeds  more than abrasions the other parts of  the body began to regain a sense of  mental clarity as I trusted an  unfamiliar intuition with slightly more  assurance in my voice I explained to her  that I wanted to check the vision in her  other eye I gently peeled apart her  right eyelid everything looked intact  can you see me I asked I could feel her  not had not an affirmative gesture what  do you think is gonna happen to me am I  gonna lose my eye Brianna asked  desperately the compression had  stabilized the bleeding from the eye  abrasion yet I knew my most important  training was now being called upon  anyone can compress a wound or check a  pulse but Brianna needed more than pure  procedural knowledge she needed me to  hold her pain with her to comfort and  assure her on a deeper level than any  words could convey as I clutched her  head with one hand and held her hand  with the other I felt a grip it was a  grip of fear a grip of trust a grip  between two strangers who needed each  other  the paramedics entered the plane the  tension began to ease in my body as I  calmly explained what happened and my  belief that Breanna needed emergency eye  surgery as Breanna was carted off the  plane I gave her hand a final squeeze of  reassurance before I let go I returned  to my seat and looked down to find  Briana's blood still clinging to my  shirt I sat through the rest of the  flight in a shaky stunned silence off  the flight a slew of phone calls with  power parents and Confused friends  didn't help they didn't quite get it but  I'm not sure I did at that point either  three weeks later I peeled the plastic  film of my freshly dry-cleaned white  shirt pure white that was the last of  the reminders at this point not much  remained the vivid flashbacks had ceased  the bloodstains were washed away and  through a heartwarming inbox message I  had learned to surgeries later even  Brianna had recovered I we folded this  shirt and placed it into the drawer and  flopped onto my duvet I scrolled through  Netflix the new episode of my favorite  mindless reality TV show had not been  released  probably a sign I should go to bed I  closed my eyes and before I could  contemplate anything of substance my  body was overcome with sleep I was back  to that cold December morning on the  tarmac of O'Hare Airport my call button  was illuminated as I heard the  announcement for the healthcare  professional to present to the front of  the plane yet I could not move my body  was paralyzed and sheer panic I fumbled  around for the frayed familiar edges of  that card I prized so dearly I pulled it  out and set it on my tray table the worn  edges were replaced by crisp pointed  corners and it's soiled gray color now  gleamed a pure and assertive white as I  read the card I noticed that the date of  issue was now different it read K award  L R on December 3rd 2018 I put the card  back in my wallet with a sense of pride  I felt the control washed back into my  limbs as I stood up and confidently  walked down  this time I knew what it meant to be a  nurse  [Applause]  I used to say that nurses or secret  keepers we kept our experiences of the  most intense intimate moment in people's  lives cloaked to all but other nurses  but tonight you are privileged to hear  three remarkable nurse writers disclose  their story they are our future and they  are taught at Yale that there is an  expectation that they will be leaders  Mavericks that will make a difference we  need to challenge ourselves to continue  writing about our work tonight you heard  how the anatomy lab is where our  students meet their first patients and  fall in love with the complexity the  challenge and the charity of our donors  that none of us have progressed to where  we are alone whatever we have built is  on the ground that was leveled by those  who came before us some many generations  long ago for others they are the first  and finally the bravery the grit to  enter another's world and simply try to  help knowing that a license will never  prepare you for all the challenges that  are to come the dedication to caring  about people fuels their boldness these  students are the future of our  profession and they will continue to  write about our work as a reminder to  all of us here tonight that we are  incredibly lucky to have these  remarkable people in our profession so  we look forward to seeing you next year  as we eagerly anticipate our students  ability to enchant to inform and to  exclaim that Nursing indeed does matter  thank you very much  [Applause]  [Music]
 

